From 5363ef006bcb73187731413bd2f3bb3dcbd1d495 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 15:53:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 64/64] External clock: a clock byte read late ticks at once,
 not 2.9 ms late, and samples rendered ahead don't delay it

inputTick() placed the tick (time - i2sTXBufferPos) / 8 + 40 samples ahead, modulo the output buffer's 128: 168
samples after the byte arrived, as long as the byte is read at the start of the audio routine, with nothing rendered
ahead, and arrived at most 40 samples before the buffer was last filled (the non-task-manager build reads MIDI there).
With the task manager the playback routine reads it in its own task: a byte that arrived longer before (it waited more
than the samples due plus 40) wrapped to ~128 samples late, and the samples rendered ahead made every tick that much
later. v17 made both more likely (the playback routine every 16 samples, e7eb4e34; renders of 60 samples, ffdebd79).

Now an arrival past the DMA's current place counts as before the buffer was last filled, the samples rendered ahead
come off, and a byte too late for its place ticks at once. Exact for a byte read within 128 samples of its arrival.

tests/sdload clockin (new: 120 BPM clock bytes through the DIN MIDI input with the RZ/A1's timing capture, S4's song,
typical card), tick placement error |latency - 168| in samples, mean / max / ticks over 64 late, of 190:
  v16            0.5 / 4.6 / 0,    with UI 1.9 / 127.4 / 2
  v17 before     3.2 / 127.4 / 2,  with UI 2.2 / 127.4 / 1
  now            1.4 / 14.1 / 0,   with UI 1.5 / 21.6 / 0
The rest: bytes read so late (up to 66 samples) that, with up to 29 samples rendered ahead, their place had gone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Eb214cM1zi1k5Gpcn9RLKh
---
 src/deluge/playback/playback_handler.cpp | 24 +++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/deluge/playback/playback_handler.cpp b/src/deluge/playback/playback_handler.cpp
index fa946422..9c58f89d 100644
--- a/src/deluge/playback/playback_handler.cpp
+++ b/src/deluge/playback/playback_handler.cpp
@@ -81,6 +81,7 @@
 extern "C" {
 #include "RZA1/gpio/gpio.h"
 #include "RZA1/uart/sio_char.h"
+#include "drivers/ssi/ssi.h"
 }
 
 PlaybackHandler playbackHandler{};
@@ -1740,11 +1741,28 @@ void PlaybackHandler::inputTick(bool fromTriggerClock, uint32_t time) {
 
 	// The 40 here is a fine-tuned amount to stop everything wrapping wrong when CPU load heavy. 28 to 98 seemed to work
 	// correctly
+	// mastertune-v17: the tick plays 168 samples (these 40 and the output buffer's 128) after the byte arrived; `time`
+	// is where the output DMA read then. Upstream took that relative to where the next rendered sample goes into the
+	// buffer (i2sTXBufferPos), + 40, modulo the buffer's 128 samples: right as long as the byte is read at the start of
+	// the audio routine, with nothing rendered ahead, and arrived at most 40 samples before the buffer was last filled.
+	// With the task manager it's read in the playback routine's own task, any time: a byte that arrived longer before
+	// wrapped to ~128 samples (2.9 ms) late, and the samples rendered ahead (up to ~28 with the minimum window) made
+	// the tick that much later. Now an arrival past the DMA's current place is taken as before the buffer was last
+	// filled (the byte waited longer than the samples due), the samples rendered ahead come off, and a byte too late
+	// for its place ticks at once. Exact for a byte read within 128 samples of its arrival (the DMA's place can't tell
+	// longer).
 	if (time) {
-		timeTilInputTick =
-		    (((uint32_t)(time - (uint32_t)AudioEngine::i2sTXBufferPos) >> (2 + NUM_MONO_OUTPUT_CHANNELS_MAGNITUDE))
-		     + 40)
+		uint32_t writePos = (uint32_t)AudioEngine::i2sTXBufferPos;
+		int32_t arrived =
+		    ((uint32_t)(time - writePos) >> (2 + NUM_MONO_OUTPUT_CHANNELS_MAGNITUDE)) & (SSI_TX_BUFFER_NUM_SAMPLES - 1);
+		int32_t due =
+		    ((uint32_t)((uint32_t)getTxBufferCurrentPlace() - writePos) >> (2 + NUM_MONO_OUTPUT_CHANNELS_MAGNITUDE))
 		    & (SSI_TX_BUFFER_NUM_SAMPLES - 1);
+		if (arrived > due) {
+			arrived -= SSI_TX_BUFFER_NUM_SAMPLES;
+		}
+		int32_t til = arrived + 40 - AudioEngine::getNumSamplesLeftToOutputFromPreviousRender();
+		timeTilInputTick = std::max<int32_t>(til, 0);
 	}
 	else {
 		timeTilInputTick = 0;
-- 
